-- #414: two ⊑-incomparable overlapping impls are correctly rejected with
-- T-CONFLICTING-IMPL — this fixture pins the RANGE.  It used to be a fabricated
-- file-start caret (0:0) on every coherence conflict, which is unusable for an
-- editor/LSP consumer: the message names the heads, but nothing points at either
-- impl.  The range must now span the head tycon of the LATER-declared impl
-- (`Pair` on the `impl C (Pair a Int)` line) — which is also the head the message
-- names FIRST, so caret and wording agree.
data Pair a b = Pair a b

interface C a where
  c : a -> Int

impl C (Pair Int b) where
  c _ = 1

impl C (Pair a Int) where
  c _ = 2

main : <IO> Unit
main = println (c (Pair 1 2))

-- ⚠️ THIS NOTE IS DELIBERATELY AT THE BOTTOM.  The golden above pins the
-- T-CONFLICTING-IMPL RANGE by absolute line number, so a note added to the header
-- would shift it and conflate "the caret moved" with the change that actually
-- happened here.  (test/AGENTS.md's fixture-line-count trap, paid for on 2026-07-31.)
--
-- Since #1155 (F-3c) this file emits TWO diagnostics and the golden carries both.
-- The second one is the pre-existing T-CONFLICTING-IMPL this fixture is about; the
-- FIRST is a goal-site T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE at `c (Pair 1 2)` on the line above,
-- because the ground goal `C (Pair Int Int)` matches `impl C (Pair Int b)` and
-- `impl C (Pair a Int)`, which are ⊑-incomparable -- DICT-SEMANTICS §6 C1's
-- "ambiguous overlap", now reported at the goal instead of silently taking the
-- first-declared impl.  Both are correct and they are different rules at different
-- sites (declaration-time condition (a) vs per-goal condition (c)); see
-- `reportAmbiguousOverlap` in compiler/types/typecheck.mdk for why the goal-site one
-- is NOT suppressed when the declaration-time one fires.
